    definition: 'Transformation Demand™ comes from: organizations, governments, hospitals, universities, private equity, public companies — entities with Transformation Packets™ requiring execution. Transformation Supply™ comes from: employees, AI agents, Certified Practitioners™, independent experts, consulting firms, automation platforms, academic institutions — entities with measured capabilities and Transformation Reputation™. The Marketplace is powered by the Transformation Matching Engine™ (TI-033) and governed by Transformation Reputation™.',
    operatingLaw: 'Every completed packet generates: Evidence, Lessons, Reusable Templates, Updated Knowledge, Improved AI Models, Institutional Memory, Future Recommendations. Transformation therefore compounds — each completed packet makes future packets faster, cheaper, and higher probability of success.',
    implications: [
      'The Transformation Marketplace™ is the Phase 3 commercial architecture of Leider Capital — a constitutionally governed platform connecting transformation demand to transformation supply at scale. It is ratified now and built deliberately when Phase 1 and Phase 2 products achieve commercial traction.',
      'Certified Practitioners™ are a new category of transformation professional — people whose capabilities are verified through the Capability Graph™ and whose reputation is earned through measured transformation outcomes rather than credentials alone.',
      'The Transformation Cloud™ — aggregating anonymized execution patterns across organizations — is the network effect that makes the Marketplace constitutionally defensible. As more organizations contribute execution data, matching quality improves for all participants.',
      'Transformation Marketplace™ completes the constitutional Opportunity Hierarchy™: Market → Opportunity Zones™ → Company → Unlock Potential™ → Equity Reclamation™ → Mechanism → Blueprint™ → Transformation Compiler™ → Packets™ → Marketplace™ → Realized Value. The full chain from discovery to execution is now constitutionally defined.',
    ],
